{
  "case_id": "2006-06-102",
  "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Just on the second point. I wanted -- does the rule of the Federal district court in forum non conveniens where it dismisses a case for lack of a convenient form, does it have any ongoing consequences as the law of the case? I know our Chick Kam Choo precedent where we said Federal court determination forum non conveniens is not binding in a state court, but supposing in this case that the parties went to San Francisco with a -- and said we want to sue there. Would the San Francisco court, the United States District Court feel bound by the determination"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Do you know any case, Mr. Castanias, where there has been a forum non conveniens dismissal in favor of a foreign forum where the plaintiff has then attempted to go into a different U.S. court to get a different resolution?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Sinochem International",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The complaint that Malaysia International Shipping makes against Sinochem is nothing more than a claim that Sinochem defrauded a Chinese court. There is a term used in the complaint, a fraudulent misrepresentation. The term negligent misrepresentation is also used. But make no mistake about it. The complaint in this case is that Sinochem made a misrepresentation to Chinese courts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0000-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HALLWARD-DRIEMEIER: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: In Steel Company the Court held that it is impermissible to adjudicate the merits of a case over which the Court may lack jurisdiction. But it is another thing entirely for the Court to decline to exercise jurisdiction that it might well possess. In Ruhrgas clarified, in language that counsel has already quoted, that a court may choose among threshold grounds for denying audience to a case on the merits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0000-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Forum non conveniens is in the nature of an abstention extension doctrine. In Gulf Oil, one of the first cases of this Court to describe the forum non conveniens doctrine and its factors, analogizes forum non conveniens to a Burford abstention. The Court even in Steel Company acknowledged that abstention on grounds of Younger, for example, would be permissible to decide before resolving a disputed question of jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Higgins",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We believe that this Court should affirm the judgment of the Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit based on three reasons."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "whether if the Court does dismiss on the basis of forum non conveniens without first addressing personal jurisdiction, it must assume the existence of personal jurisdiction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Castanias",
      "case_id": "2006-06-102",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ac5c555e75bebbf2e250e224612b0f1ca80440c8bbe3dcb7ed35ca4d84943095",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ac5c555e75be-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Castanias: Mr. Chief Justice, unless the Court has further questions, we'll rest on the argument given in the briefs."
    },
    {
